\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22\lang9 In a typical liberal democracy, the executive is one of\par
the three key branches of government (alongside the\par
legislature and judiciary), all of which interact with\par
each other viaa process of checks and balances. Within\par
UK politics, the cabinet has become established as one of\par
the executive branch\rquote s central features. It is a collection\par
of senior ministers, headed by the prime minister (PM),\par
without which effective political administration could\par
arguably not operate or function (Box 1).\par
In a historical context, the first notable cabinet\par
ministers emerged as advisers to the monarchs Charles I\par
and Charles IT in the mid-seventeenth century, either\par
side of the English Civil War. This reflected cautious\par
democratic and political change that demanded that\par
both Parliament and government ministers be able to\par
control and regulate royal powers. It also reflected the\par
increasing complexity of governing the country, not all\par
of which could be practically dealt with by an often ill-\par
prepared monarch.\par
\par
***************************************************************************\par
\par
The evolution of the cabinet\par
While specific ministerial roles steadily evolved in\par
offering important political advice to gradually less\par
powerful monarchs, Robert Walpole is considered to\par
have been the first formal prime minister, operating\par
under Kings George 1 and II between 1721 and\par
1742. As the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries\par
progressed and further democratic change developed,\par
the constitutional historian Walter Bagehot described\par
the cabinet as the \lquote near fusion\rquote  of the executive and\par
legislature, \lquote a hyphen which joins... a buckle which\par
fastens\rquote  and the \lquote efficient secret\rquote  of the British political\par
system (1867, The English Constitution).\par
Such words further illustrated the cabinet\rquote s perceived\par
importance for effective government. It was not until\par
1937 that the Ministers of the Crown Act formally\par
acknowledged cabiner ministers and justified them\par
with a specific salary for their efforts. As the twentieth\par
century progressed, the cabinet grew in prominence\par
and importance.\par
\par
***************************************************************************\par
\par
Cabinet composition\par
Prime ministers can vary cabinet\rquote s size and\par
composition, and there is no rule as to the number\par
of members required. In 2016 Theresa May appointed\par
a specific cabinet position heading the newly created\par
Department for Exiting the European Union (DEXEU).\par
A cabinet will generally contain between 20 and\par
30 members, with each member usually having a\par
ministerial department to run. Those appointed to\par
cabinet roles tend to be fairly experienced and senior\par
politicians, and are usually from the same political\par
party, although not when there is a coalition, as was the\par
case between 2010 and 2015 when the Conservatives\par
and Liberal Democrats were in government together.\par
Modern prime ministers will try to ensure their\par
cabinet has a balanced composition in terms of gender,\par
ethnicity, regional representation, party factions, etc.\par
The PM will often use their powers of patronage to\par
appoint key allies and friends to important positions, as\par
they know they will be loyal. Tony Blair's relationship\par
with Peter Mandelson during the \lquote New Labour\rquote  era is\par
a good example.\par
Boris Johnson's first selection of cabinet colleagues\par
created a cabinet that was more diverse in terms of\par
representing various key groups of British society than\par
past cabinets, although in political terms it was highly\par
right wing and Eurosceptic.\par
\par
***************************************************************************\par
\par
Presidential PMs\par
Although the prime minister is often viewed as \lquote first\par
among equals\rquote  in relation to their cabinet, academic\par
scholars such as Michael Foley have written about\par
the rise of the \lquote British presidency\rquote , focusing on the\par
leadership styles of Margaret Thatcher and Tony Blair in\par
office. Observers have noted that such powerful prime\par
ministers, armed with large parliamentary majorities,\par
operated in an excessively dominant manner via a\par
process of spatial leadership.\par
This analysis suggests that these PMs acted more like\par
presidents \f1\emdash  a role that appears far more important\par
and significant than anyone else in the political\par
system. However, in constitutional terms, the UK is not\par
presidential but parliamentary, and prime ministers\par
have significant limitations on what they can do, which\par
principally stem from their cabinet.\par
Professor George Jones claims that some prime\par
ministers push the \lquote elasticity\rquote  of the role too far, i.e.\par
they overstretch their powers and are brought back into\par
line by the collective power of cabinet.\par
\par
\f0 ***************************************************************************\f1\par
\par
Prime ministerial-cabinet relations\par
Controversial policies can undermine a prime\par
minister\rquote s authority, e.g. Thatcher and the poll tax\par
or Blair and Iraq. Personality and policy clashes can\par
develop between powerful and ambitious rivals, e.g.\par
Thatcher vs Heseltine, Blairvs Brown, Mayvs Johnson.\par
Long-serving PMs can build up both ministerial and\par
parliamentary enemies within their party. Indeed, it is\par
considered dangerous to have too many political \lquote big\par
beasts\rquote  outside of the cabinet. Political and ideological\par
splits within the party can spread to the very top,\par
with a recent example being May and Brexit, which\par
poisoned intra-cabinet relations.\par
A divided and disunited cabinet is often ineffective,\par
while increased media focus on the prime minister\par
has sidelined cabinet\rquote s influence. Cabinets can\par
ultimately be bypassed by dominant leaders with large\par
parliamentary majorities. Tony Blair is often cited as\par
the classic example, as he appeared to override cabinet\par
concerns over issues as diverse as the Iraq War and the\par
Millennium Dome. Similarly, Thatcher removed many\par
\lquote wets' from her Cabinet over the years, until it became\par
dominated by loyalists (although they still turned\par
on her in 1990, largely dismayed by her increasingly\par
dictatorial approach)\par
Tony Blair utilised sofa politics to deal with cabinet\par
opposition, where he would meet ministers on a less\par
formal, one-to-one basis to thrash out a specific policy\par
position before it got to full cabinet. Powerful and\par
unelected advisors such as Alastair Campbell under Tony\par
Blair, or Fiona Hill and Nick Timothy under Theresa\par
May, can often weaken cabinet influence. It has been\par
suggested that Dominic Cummings may enjoy similarly\par
potent powers as a special advisor under Boris Johnson,\par
as evidenced by the dramatic cabinet reshuffle in\par
February 2020 (see Table 1)\par
\par
\f0 ***************************************************************************\f1\par
\par
Cabinet unity\par
Over recent history, effective prime ministers have\par
accepted the role of the cabinet in order to secure what\par
they want to achieve. John Major (1990-97) revived\par
collective government in the aftermath of Thatcher\rquote s\par
more dominant and autocratic approach. This was\par
widely viewed as a positive development that initially\par
made the executive more productive. Tony Blair\par
appointed John Prescott (a figure from \lquote Old Labour\rquote )\par
as deputy prime minister between 1997 and 2007, and\par
this relationship became a mechanism for maintaining\par
broad cabinet and party unity.\par
In a similar vein, David Cameron appointed Nick\par
Clegg as his deputy, primarily to keep the Liberal\par
Democrats onside during the coalition years. Such\par
prominent deputies chaired many cabinet committees,\par
and this role subsequently became an important strategic one\par
to maintain government focus and stability. Powerful and\par
relatively successful prime ministers ultimately accept the\par
need to delegate to trusted loyalists in order to get things done,\par
which is something that Boris Johnson should note.\par
\par
\f0 ***************************************************************************\par
\f1 Conclusion\par
The role and significance of the cabinet within a changing\par
executive model has evolved, developed and changed\par
in influence throughout the postwar era. Scholars such\par
as Dunleavy and Rhodes (1990) have referred to a \lquote core\par
executive\rquote  rather than a cabinet structure, which reflects\par
the addition of non-ministerial and unelected figures to the\par
inner circle of executive power.\par
While the UK prime minister's status has risen during this\par
era of intense media scrutiny fuelled by 24-hour news, the\par
internet and social media, cabinet maintains key powers to\par
control and rein in anyone who seeks to stretch the \lquote elasticity\rquote\par
of the prime ministerial role. Several recent PMs have seen\par
such cabinet-level power lead to their downfall, and on this\par
basis the latest incumbent of 10 Downing Street should tread\par
carefully if he wishes to make a success of the role\par
